WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.329 --> 00:00:04.279
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.279 --> 00:00:09.710
Today we are going to talk about a paper that
builds on a previous work by the name Deep

00:00:09.710 --> 00:00:12.719
Image Priors, DIP in short.

00:00:12.719 --> 00:00:18.100
This work was capable of performing JPEG compression
artifact removal, image inpainting, or in

00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:26.519
other words, filling in parts of the image
with data that makes sense, super resolution,

00:00:26.519 --> 00:00:28.199
and image denoising.

00:00:28.199 --> 00:00:29.539
It was quite the package.

00:00:29.539 --> 00:00:35.190
This new method is able to subdivide an image
into a collection of layers, which makes it

00:00:35.190 --> 00:00:41.160
capable of doing many seemingly unrelated
tasks, for instance, one, it can do image

00:00:41.160 --> 00:00:46.570
segmentation, which typically means producing
a mask that shows us the boundaries between

00:00:46.570 --> 00:00:48.820
the foreground and background.

00:00:48.820 --> 00:00:52.720
As an additional advantage, it can also do
this for videos as well.

00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:58.770
Two, it can perform dehazing, which can also
be thought of as a decomposition task where

00:00:58.770 --> 00:01:04.440
the input is one image, and the output is
an image with haze, and one with the objects

00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:06.410
hiding behind the haze.

00:01:06.410 --> 00:01:11.710
If you spend a tiny bit of time looking out
the window on a hazy day, you will immediately

00:01:11.710 --> 00:01:17.060
see that this is immensely difficult, mostly
because of the fact that the amount of haze

00:01:17.060 --> 00:01:20.500
that we see is non-uniform along the landscape.

00:01:20.500 --> 00:01:26.930
The AI has to detect and remove just the right
amount of this haze and recover the original

00:01:26.930 --> 00:01:28.780
colors of the image.

00:01:28.780 --> 00:01:35.570
And three, it can also subdivide these crazy
examples where two images are blended together.

00:01:35.570 --> 00:01:39.920
In a moment, I’ll show you a better example
with a complex texture where it is easier

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:43.060
to see the utility of such a technique.

00:01:43.060 --> 00:01:49.440
And four, of course, it can also perform image
inpainting, which, for instance, can help

00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:54.220
us remove watermarks or other unwanted artifacts
from our photos.

00:01:54.220 --> 00:01:59.542
This case can also be thought of an image
layer plus a watermark layer, and in the end,

00:01:59.542 --> 00:02:02.760
the algorithm is able to recover both of them.

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:08.009
As you see here on the right, a tiny part
of the content seems to bleed into the watermark

00:02:08.009 --> 00:02:11.230
layer, but the results are still amazing.

00:02:11.230 --> 00:02:17.000
It does this by using multiple of these DIPs,
deep image prior networks, and goes by the

00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:18.840
name DoubleDIP.

00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:21.350
That one got me good when I’ve first seen
it.

00:02:21.350 --> 00:02:27.260
You see here how it tries to reproduce this
complex textured pattern as a sum of these

00:02:27.260 --> 00:02:30.480
two, much simpler individual components.

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:35.630
The supplementary materials are available
right in your browser, and show you a ton

00:02:35.630 --> 00:02:38.660
of comparisons against other previous works.

00:02:38.660 --> 00:02:44.520
Here you see results from these earlier works
on image dehazing and see that indeed, the

00:02:44.520 --> 00:02:47.000
new results are second to none.

00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:49.900
And all this progress within only two years.

00:02:49.900 --> 00:02:52.150
What a time to be alive!

00:02:52.150 --> 00:02:56.160
If like me, you love information theory, woo-hoo!

00:02:56.160 --> 00:02:59.700
Make sure to have a look at the paper and
you’ll be a happy person.

